<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    Doctrine mapping for the `Identity` context's SECOND aggregate root.

    It needs no config change: `config/packages/doctrine.yaml` maps the whole
    `Infrastructure/Identity/Persistence/Doctrine/mapping` directory under the `Identity` block, so
    dropping a file in here is all it takes. That is one mapping block per bounded context
    (ADR-0007 decision 3) paying off the first time it is tested.

    Everything ADR-0007 established for `User.orm.xml` applies unchanged — XML so that no
    `use Doctrine\...` lands in `Domain/`, explicit table and column names so that renaming a PHP
    property can never silently rename a production column, `<generator strategy="NONE"/>` because
    ids are minted by the repository, `datetimetz_immutable` so a stored instant is absolute. What
    follows is only what is *new* here.

    WHY `user_id` IS A PLAIN TYPED COLUMN AND NOT A `<many-to-one>` ASSOCIATION (AC-34).

    The reflex — and what every Doctrine tutorial writes — is
    `<many-to-one field="user" target-entity="User"/>`. It is wrong here, and for a modelling
    reason rather than a performance one. `EmailVerificationRequest` holds a `UserId`, not a
    `User`: aggregates reference each other **by identity**, so that no caller can reach across an
    aggregate boundary and mutate a second root through the first. An association would put a real
    `User` object (or a lazy proxy of one) on this aggregate and hand exactly that power to anyone
    holding a request. It would also let Doctrine's cascade settings quietly decide transactional
    semantics that ADR-0009 decision 3 argued out explicitly: the whole point of two aggregates
    here is that redemption is *two* saves in a deliberate order, not one cascade.

    The mapping therefore does what the Domain already does — stores the identity, typed. Reusing
    the existing `identity_user_id` DBAL type (rather than a bare `guid`) means the column still
    round-trips a real `UserId` value object, so nothing is lost except the association.

    WHY THERE IS NO FOREIGN KEY TO `identity_user` (ADR-0009 decision 4).

    Follows from the same idea, and is worth stating in the mapping because a future reader will
    otherwise "fix" it in a migration. Referential integrity *between aggregates* is the
    application's job, not the schema's. And a hand-added FK that Doctrine's mapping does not know
    about is permanent noise: every subsequent `make migration.make` would diff it as an unwanted
    constraint and offer to drop it, so it would be deleted-and-re-added forever by whoever ran the
    generator last. The accepted cost is that orphan rows become possible if a user is ever
    hard-deleted — nothing deletes users today, the pruning job owed later will sweep them, and the
    GDPR-erasure conversation before public launch must not forget this table.
-->
<doctrine-mapping xmlns="http://doctrine-project.org/schemas/orm/doctrine-mapping"
                  xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
                  xsi:schemaLocation="http://doctrine-project.org/schemas/orm/doctrine-mapping
                                      https://www.doctrine-project.org/schemas/orm/doctrine-mapping.xsd">

    <entity name="App\Domain\Identity\Entity\EmailVerificationRequest"
            table="identity_email_verification_request">

        <!--
            The composite index behind `countIssuedForUserSince()` — the anti-abuse rule I-12.

            Column order is the whole design: `user_id` leads because it is matched for equality,
            `issued_at` follows because it is matched as a range. A B-tree can only use a range
            predicate on the first column after all the equality ones, so `(user_id, issued_at)`
            is an index scan over a narrow slice while `(issued_at, user_id)` would force a scan
            across every user's rows in the window. AC-37 asserts this with `EXPLAIN`, which is
            also why the index is named here rather than left to Doctrine's `IDX_<hash>` — an
            assertion cannot refer to a name nobody chose.
        -->
        <indexes>
            <index name="idx_identity_email_verification_request_user_issued" columns="user_id,issued_at"/>
        </indexes>

        <!--
            The unique index on the digest does two jobs at once, which is why it is a unique
            constraint and not a plain index.

            It serves the only hot query on this table — `findByTokenHash()`, one lookup per click
            on a verification link — and it makes a duplicate digest a database-level impossibility
            rather than a hope. Note what the adapter deliberately does *not* do with it: unlike
            `uniq_identity_user_email`, whose violation `DoctrineUserRepository::save()` translates
            into `EmailAlreadyRegistered`, a violation here is not a business event. It would mean
            two independent draws of 256 CSPRNG bits collided, and the honest answer to that is a
            500 and an alert. See `EmailVerificationRequestRepository::save()`'s docblock for the
            full argument.
        -->
        <unique-constraints>
            <unique-constraint name="uniq_identity_email_verification_request_token_hash" columns="token_hash"/>
        </unique-constraints>

        <!--
            `strategy="NONE"` — assigned by the application, minted by
            `EmailVerificationRequestRepository::nextIdentity()` before `issue()` is called. That is
            what lets the aggregate record `EmailVerificationRequested` carrying its own id without
            waiting for a post-flush sequence value, and what lets a request be built and thrown
            away on a rate-limit refusal having never met the database.
        -->
        <id name="id" type="identity_email_verification_request_id" column="id">
            <generator strategy="NONE"/>
        </id>

        <!-- Reference by identity, not by association — see the header. -->
        <field name="userId" column="user_id" type="identity_user_id" nullable="false"/>

        <!-- Length mirrors `HashedVerificationToken::MAX_LENGTH`; the VO owns the rule, the column
             enforces it. Deliberately not narrowed to SHA-256's 64 characters: the digest algorithm
             is `RandomVerificationTokenGenerator`'s choice, and a column that encodes it would have
             to be migrated the day that choice changes. -->
        <field name="tokenHash" column="token_hash" type="identity_verification_token_hash" length="255" nullable="false"/>

        <!--
            `datetimetz_immutable`, as everywhere: Postgres `TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE` stores an
            absolute instant, so a value written by the UTC `Clock` reads back as the same moment
            whatever timezone the writing container had. The type formats through `'Y-m-d H:i:sO'`
            and therefore discards microseconds before column precision is ever consulted — which is
            exactly why `Clock` mandates whole seconds (ADR-0007 decision 8) and why
            `isExpiredAt()` compares with a strict `>`: at second granularity a `>=` would shorten
            every link by up to a second.

            Both are NOT NULL because `issue()` derives them together and neither can be absent from
            a request that exists.
        -->
        <field name="issuedAt" column="issued_at" type="datetimetz_immutable" nullable="false"/>
        <field name="expiresAt" column="expires_at" type="datetimetz_immutable" nullable="false"/>

        <!--
            `redeemed_at` is the single-use property, stored as a nullable instant rather than a
            boolean flag: `NULL` means "still live" and any value means "burnt, and here is when".
            A boolean would answer the invariant (I-9) and throw away the audit trail, and the audit
            trail is half of why this challenge is an aggregate at all.
        -->
        <field name="redeemedAt" column="redeemed_at" type="datetimetz_immutable" nullable="true"/>

    </entity>

</doctrine-mapping>
